  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Warmer than average temperatures again this week.\n\nEnjoy the conditions today as we can expect another warm period for \nthis week. Similar to the start of September, a large mid-level \nridge over the Great Plains will move eastward and bring warmer and \nhumid conditions to the southeastern CONUS and the Mid-Atlantic. The \ngood news is that it won't be nearly as hot and Heat Advisories are \nvery unlikely. The bad news is it will still be unseasonably warm \nwith high temperatures in the mid-80s to upper 90s by Thursday \nafternoon. Thursday is currently forecast to be the warmest of the \nweek and the high temperatures will gradually rise each day until \nthen. A cold front is forecast to approach the region towards this \nweekend which may be able to bring temperatures down a little.\n\nKey Message 2: Another round of showers and thunderstorms possible \nlater in the week.\n\nA surface high pressure system will keep conditions dry for the \nfirst half of this work week before a cold front brings another \nround of precipitation by Thursday. The timing of the front's \npassage is a little uncertain and adds a monkey wrench into the \nforecast. Since Thursday will be the warmest of the week, it may be \nthe most unstable and the front's passage could coincide with \nfavorable timing for severe weather. The front also looks to bring \nfavorable wind shear conditions (25-35 knots) as its associated \n500mb trough moves through the region. Model guidance has the \nsoutheastern ridge breaking up into two small pieces with one \nremaining over the southeast and the other in the southwest CONUS. \nThis results in a trough covering the gap between the mid-level \nhighs and some models have vorticity and a small shortwave making \nits way around the southeastern ridge and northeastward towards the \nMid-Altantic. This too will have downstream effects on which parts \nof the forecast region will receive precipitation and could delay \nthe front's passage. For the time being, the severe threat looks to \nbe low with precipitation chances possible Thursday through \nSunday. One element of the forecast that has greater confidence \nis the increase of moisture and PWATs by the weekend. With PWAT \nvalues approaching into the 1.50-2.00 inch range, the concern \nfor heavy rainfall rates and flooding is present during the \nfront's passage. So far, the upslope nature of the storms that \nwill likely accompany the front place the mountains of WV and \nsouthwest VA at the greatest risk. Nevertheless, because of the \ntiming conditions, this forecast will likely need several \nupdates until the event's arrival.",
